1
00:00:01,360 --> 00:00:02,400
April the second.

2
00:00:02,960 --> 00:00:04,600
Meera has found her cart.

3
00:00:04,880 --> 00:00:08,640
Wheels, a stove, kettles, a stack of glasses.

4
00:00:08,720 --> 00:00:09,600
The price?

5
00:00:09,720 --> 00:00:11,440
Thirty-six thousand rupees.

6
00:00:12,588 --> 00:00:15,868
Thirty-six thousand rupees, out of the galla.

7
00:00:16,476 --> 00:00:18,748
So did Meera just lose thirty-six thousand

8
00:00:18,828 --> 00:00:19,628
rupees?

9
00:00:25,489 --> 00:00:27,849
First, last lesson's answers.

10
00:00:28,129 --> 00:00:28,449
One.

11
00:00:28,849 --> 00:00:31,089
Gopal Dairy delivers eight thousand rupees

12
00:00:31,169 --> 00:00:33,529
of milk, and Meera will pay later.

13
00:00:33,809 --> 00:00:35,729
The stall now has eight thousand rupees

14
00:00:35,809 --> 00:00:38,609
of stock — milk and supplies to sell.

15
00:00:38,689 --> 00:00:39,689
That's an asset.

16
00:00:40,489 --> 00:00:43,209
And it owes Gopal eight thousand rupees.

17
00:00:43,289 --> 00:00:44,489
That's a liability.

18
00:00:44,953 --> 00:00:45,353
Two.

19
00:00:45,833 --> 00:00:47,273
Meera's personal scooter?

20
00:00:47,753 --> 00:00:49,273
Not an asset of the stall.

21
00:00:49,433 --> 00:00:50,353
It's hers.

22
00:00:50,553 --> 00:00:52,713
The stall is its own person.

23
00:00:53,159 --> 00:00:53,799
Three.

24
00:00:54,039 --> 00:00:56,519
The stall has eighty thousand, and owes

25
00:00:56,679 --> 00:00:57,799
thirty thousand.

26
00:00:57,879 --> 00:01:00,679
So Meera's share is fifty thousand rupees.

27
00:01:01,730 --> 00:01:04,290
Last time, we said it in one sentence.

28
00:01:04,530 --> 00:01:07,330
What you have equals what you owe, plus

29
00:01:07,570 --> 00:01:08,530
what's yours.

30
00:01:09,370 --> 00:01:11,130
Today, let's build it.

31
00:01:11,370 --> 00:01:14,090
Meet the taraazu — a balance scale.

32
00:01:14,670 --> 00:01:17,230
On the left pan go the assets — everything

33
00:01:17,270 --> 00:01:18,430
the stall has.

34
00:01:19,010 --> 00:01:21,570
On the right pan go liabilities and equity

35
00:01:21,970 --> 00:01:24,210
— everyone with a claim on those assets.

36
00:01:24,776 --> 00:01:25,416
Watch.

37
00:01:25,656 --> 00:01:27,736
Meera puts in fifty thousand.

38
00:01:27,856 --> 00:01:30,536
Cash on the left, her equity on the right.

39
00:01:30,616 --> 00:01:31,296
Level.

40
00:01:32,176 --> 00:01:34,416
Ravi Mama lends thirty thousand.

41
00:01:34,456 --> 00:01:36,656
The left grows to eighty thousand, and his

42
00:01:36,736 --> 00:01:38,256
loan lands on the right.

43
00:01:38,336 --> 00:01:39,216
Level again.

44
00:01:40,256 --> 00:01:42,816
Eighty thousand equals thirty thousand plus

45
00:01:42,896 --> 00:01:44,016
fifty thousand.

46
00:01:44,096 --> 00:01:46,176
This is the accounting equation.

47
00:01:47,016 --> 00:01:49,736
Assets equal liabilities plus equity.

48
00:01:50,985 --> 00:01:52,665
Now, back to the cart.

49
00:01:52,825 --> 00:01:55,305
Thirty-six thousand rupees left the galla.

50
00:01:55,385 --> 00:01:57,065
Did the stall lose money?

51
00:01:57,145 --> 00:01:59,145
Here's a question we'll ask every single

52
00:01:59,225 --> 00:02:00,585
time money moves.

53
00:02:00,665 --> 00:02:02,505
Which two things changed?

54
00:02:04,693 --> 00:02:05,173
One.

55
00:02:05,413 --> 00:02:08,213
Cash went down by thirty-six thousand.

56
00:02:08,373 --> 00:02:08,853
Two.

57
00:02:08,933 --> 00:02:11,493
The stall now has equipment worth thirty-six

58
00:02:11,653 --> 00:02:12,533
thousand.

59
00:02:12,613 --> 00:02:14,933
Both are assets, so both sit on the left

60
00:02:15,053 --> 00:02:15,653
pan.

61
00:02:16,233 --> 00:02:18,793
One goes down, one goes up — and the scale

62
00:02:18,873 --> 00:02:19,913
doesn't move.

63
00:02:19,993 --> 00:02:21,673
Still eighty thousand.

64
00:02:21,833 --> 00:02:23,433
Meera didn't lose money.

65
00:02:24,053 --> 00:02:26,253
She turned cash into a cart.

66
00:02:26,293 --> 00:02:28,093
The stall isn't worth any less.

67
00:02:28,133 --> 00:02:30,493
It just holds its money in a different shape.

68
00:02:31,052 --> 00:02:32,412
Here's where people slip.

69
00:02:32,892 --> 00:02:35,652
She spent thirty-six thousand — so that's

70
00:02:35,692 --> 00:02:36,732
an expense!

71
00:02:37,854 --> 00:02:38,374
Nope.

72
00:02:38,574 --> 00:02:41,614
An expense is money that's used up and gone.

73
00:02:41,854 --> 00:02:43,014
The cart isn't gone.

74
00:02:43,214 --> 00:02:45,134
It's standing right there, ready to make

75
00:02:45,294 --> 00:02:45,934
chai.

76
00:02:46,654 --> 00:02:49,174
It will wear out, slowly — and we'll deal

77
00:02:49,254 --> 00:02:50,854
with that at the end of the month.

78
00:02:51,907 --> 00:02:55,507
Same day, Meera buys tea, milk and sugar.

79
00:02:55,587 --> 00:02:58,547
Six thousand rupees of stock, paid in cash.

80
00:02:58,627 --> 00:03:00,227
Which two things changed?

81
00:03:02,307 --> 00:03:03,827
Meera's got the first one.

82
00:03:03,907 --> 00:03:06,147
Stock goes up by six thousand.

83
00:03:06,227 --> 00:03:07,427
Can you name the second?

84
00:03:09,786 --> 00:03:11,786
Cash goes down by six thousand.

85
00:03:12,266 --> 00:03:13,706
Another swap on the left.

86
00:03:13,946 --> 00:03:16,746
The scale stays level, at eighty thousand.

87
00:03:17,889 --> 00:03:19,689
Now you, on your own.

88
00:03:19,969 --> 00:03:20,849
April the third.

89
00:03:21,329 --> 00:03:23,569
Gopal Dairy delivers eight thousand rupees

90
00:03:23,649 --> 00:03:25,809
of milk and supplies — the delivery from

91
00:03:25,889 --> 00:03:27,009
last lesson's question.

92
00:03:27,409 --> 00:03:28,689
Meera will pay later.

93
00:03:28,929 --> 00:03:31,169
You already know the two changes: stock

94
00:03:31,249 --> 00:03:33,409
up by eight thousand, and eight thousand

95
00:03:33,569 --> 00:03:34,369
owed to Gopal.

96
00:03:34,849 --> 00:03:37,089
So here's the real question — what does

97
00:03:37,249 --> 00:03:39,249
each side of the scale read now?

98
00:03:42,403 --> 00:03:44,723
Stock goes up by eight thousand, on the

99
00:03:44,883 --> 00:03:45,523
left.

100
00:03:46,003 --> 00:03:48,963
For a moment, the scale tips… then the right

101
00:03:49,043 --> 00:03:50,083
pan catches up.

102
00:03:50,323 --> 00:03:53,283
Meera now owes Gopal eight thousand rupees.

103
00:03:54,003 --> 00:03:55,803
Eighty-eight thousand equals thirty-eight

104
00:03:55,963 --> 00:03:57,963
thousand plus fifty thousand.

105
00:03:58,043 --> 00:03:58,683
Level.

106
00:03:59,932 --> 00:04:01,292
So here's the big idea.

107
00:04:01,852 --> 00:04:04,172
Every transaction has two effects.

108
00:04:05,012 --> 00:04:07,732
Every rupee comes from somewhere, and goes

109
00:04:07,812 --> 00:04:08,452
somewhere.

110
00:04:08,692 --> 00:04:11,092
Accountants call this the dual aspect.

111
00:04:11,660 --> 00:04:14,620
And it only ever plays out in four ways.

112
00:04:15,460 --> 00:04:18,180
Both sides go up — like Ravi Mama's loan.

113
00:04:18,580 --> 00:04:21,340
Both sides go down — like paying off a bill.

114
00:04:21,540 --> 00:04:24,740
A swap on the left — cash turns into a cart.

115
00:04:24,860 --> 00:04:27,460
Or a swap on the right — one claim turns

116
00:04:27,580 --> 00:04:28,300
into another.

117
00:04:28,500 --> 00:04:29,980
You'll meet this one in May.

118
00:04:31,060 --> 00:04:34,340
In every case, the scale never stays tipped.

119
00:04:35,597 --> 00:04:35,997
So.

120
00:04:36,317 --> 00:04:39,357
The scale: assets on the left, liabilities

121
00:04:39,517 --> 00:04:41,037
plus equity on the right.

122
00:04:41,517 --> 00:04:43,717
Every transaction has two effects.

123
00:04:43,917 --> 00:04:46,797
And all four patterns keep the scale level.

124
00:04:47,931 --> 00:04:48,651
Your turn.

125
00:04:48,811 --> 00:04:49,811
Three quick ones.

126
00:04:50,411 --> 00:04:50,931
One.

127
00:04:51,211 --> 00:04:53,371
What does the equation read after Gopal's

128
00:04:53,451 --> 00:04:54,211
delivery?

129
00:04:56,291 --> 00:04:56,851
Two.

130
00:04:57,091 --> 00:04:58,971
Meera buys a cart for cash.

131
00:04:59,171 --> 00:05:00,411
Does the scale move?

132
00:05:02,491 --> 00:05:03,051
Three.

133
00:05:03,291 --> 00:05:06,011
Meera pays back part of Ravi Mama's loan.

134
00:05:06,091 --> 00:05:07,771
Which of the four patterns is that?

135
00:05:09,811 --> 00:05:11,491
Answers next lesson.

136
00:05:12,947 --> 00:05:14,861
And now, Checkpoint One.

137
00:05:15,061 --> 00:05:16,541
A brand-new business.

138
00:05:16,741 --> 00:05:18,581
This is Aman, Meera's friend.

139
00:05:18,661 --> 00:05:20,541
He's starting a samosa cart.

140
00:05:21,321 --> 00:05:21,841
One.

141
00:05:22,121 --> 00:05:24,201
Aman puts in twenty thousand rupees of his

142
00:05:24,281 --> 00:05:25,001
savings.

143
00:05:25,241 --> 00:05:25,681
Two.

144
00:05:25,881 --> 00:05:28,281
He takes a bank loan of ten thousand.

145
00:05:28,361 --> 00:05:29,001
Three.

146
00:05:29,081 --> 00:05:31,801
He buys a cart and fryer for eighteen thousand,

147
00:05:31,881 --> 00:05:32,921
in cash.

148
00:05:33,001 --> 00:05:33,641
Four.

149
00:05:33,721 --> 00:05:35,881
He buys three thousand rupees of potatoes,

150
00:05:35,961 --> 00:05:38,521
flour and oil from Sharma Kirana, and he'll

151
00:05:38,601 --> 00:05:39,561
pay later.

152
00:05:40,241 --> 00:05:43,121
For each one: which two things changed?

153
00:05:43,201 --> 00:05:45,401
And what does Aman's scale read after it?

154
00:05:45,801 --> 00:05:47,081
Pause the video now.

155
00:05:47,121 --> 00:05:49,201
The worksheet is linked in the description.

156
00:05:52,358 --> 00:05:52,878
Done?

157
00:05:53,078 --> 00:05:53,918
Let's check.

158
00:05:54,198 --> 00:05:54,678
One.

159
00:05:54,918 --> 00:05:57,518
Aman puts in twenty thousand of his savings.

160
00:05:57,718 --> 00:05:59,318
Cash up, capital up.

161
00:05:59,398 --> 00:06:01,798
Twenty thousand equals zero, plus twenty

162
00:06:01,878 --> 00:06:02,758
thousand.

163
00:06:03,378 --> 00:06:03,898
Two.

164
00:06:03,978 --> 00:06:06,178
He takes a ten-thousand-rupee bank loan.

165
00:06:06,378 --> 00:06:08,298
Cash up, liability up.

166
00:06:08,418 --> 00:06:10,698
Thirty thousand equals ten thousand, plus

167
00:06:10,778 --> 00:06:11,898
twenty thousand.

168
00:06:12,558 --> 00:06:13,118
Three.

169
00:06:13,198 --> 00:06:15,838
He buys a cart and fryer for eighteen thousand,

170
00:06:15,878 --> 00:06:16,718
in cash.

171
00:06:16,758 --> 00:06:18,638
A swap on the left, so the totals don't

172
00:06:18,718 --> 00:06:19,318
change.

173
00:06:20,098 --> 00:06:20,818
Four.

174
00:06:20,898 --> 00:06:22,978
Three thousand rupees of potatoes, flour

175
00:06:23,058 --> 00:06:25,658
and oil, on credit from Sharma Kirana.

176
00:06:25,858 --> 00:06:28,418
Stock up, and what he owes goes up.

177
00:06:28,498 --> 00:06:30,898
Thirty-three thousand equals thirteen thousand,

178
00:06:31,058 --> 00:06:32,338
plus twenty thousand.

179
00:06:32,418 --> 00:06:33,538
Four out of four?

180
00:06:33,618 --> 00:06:35,458
Your scale never tipped either.

181
00:06:37,159 --> 00:06:38,039
Next time.

182
00:06:38,279 --> 00:06:39,359
April the fifth.

183
00:06:39,559 --> 00:06:40,359
Rent day.

184
00:06:40,599 --> 00:06:42,999
Five thousand rupees leave the galla… and

185
00:06:43,159 --> 00:06:44,479
nothing comes back.

186
00:06:44,759 --> 00:06:46,519
Will the scale finally tip?
